C. THE TWO READINGS OF ONE DEFLECTION (derived; the continuum, then the lattice)
   the row's bend (DETECTOR: a pixel screen's centroid): the push's turn per interval e_D |V_perp| / (Q^2 S) radians
   (w gains e_D V, P's base is Q S u with |u| = Q), summed over the intervals the row dwells at each Node of its path;
   in the continuum d theta / dt = |V_perp| / (Q S c) (e_D / Q = sqrt 3 = 1 / c), the sum 2 G M / (b c^2) with G M = q / (4 pi S).
   the front's tilt (DETECTOR: a phase reading across the screen, the record's phase per pixel): c times the difference
   of the wall's delay between the rows at b and b + 1, which in the continuum is 2 k_a(b) L / sqrt(L^2 + b^2): the same
   form; at n S = d the same number as the bend. The rows then ride their own wavefront; the two are one deflection,
   Newton's 2 G M / (b c^2) (Soldner 1801; Einstein 1911), and they never add: a row bent by the push arrives at its
   pixel with the delay of its own path, and the phase gradient across the screen is the delay's gradient alone
   (the bent path's extra length is second order in the angle).
   S = 256, the pair [1, 256] (n S = d): per unit of the crowd, at b = 6 and 3, on the lattice's lines
     b = 6: the delay 10 intervals at b, 7 at b + 1; the front's tilt  45.38 Links at the screen (the continuum  2.28); the row's bend 8 Links (the continuum  2.34)
     b = 3: the delay 9 intervals at b, 12 at b + 1; the front's tilt -45.38 Links at the screen (the continuum  4.66); the row's bend 6 Links (the continuum  4.69)
   S = 1024, the pair [1, 1024] (n S = d): per unit of the crowd, at b = 6 and 3, on the lattice's lines
     b = 6: the delay 3 intervals at b, 2 at b + 1; the front's tilt  15.13 Links at the screen (the continuum  0.57); the row's bend 0 Links (the continuum  0.59)
     b = 3: the delay 2 intervals at b, 3 at b + 1; the front's tilt -15.13 Links at the screen (the continuum  1.16); the row's bend 0 Links (the continuum  1.17)
   the lattice reads the two combs (the age moment's and the flow's, in the mass's plane) and not the continuum's one number:
   the row's bend is the flow's comb (3 to 4 times the isotropic sum in the plane, the light-bending note's section 4), and
   the delay's difference between the rows at b and b + 1 is not a gradient at this fan (its sign flips from b = 6 to 3):
   the front's tilt is not readable by neighbouring rows at P = 6; it needs the dense fan (P >> r) or a shell mean. The pin
   for a run is the lattice's own two readings, the click's pixel and the click's age; the sum of the two halves never.

D. THE PIN WORLDS, THE SUN, THE HOST PRICE
   at S = 1 (series K as registered) the push captures the beam (G M / (b c^2) = 11.5 at b = 6: the register's own words,
   'nature would capture the beam'); the width S is the world's one lever on the push (the drive's Q S M), the pair [1, S]
   keeps the wall's constant equal to the push's. The table at S = 256, the pair [1, 256], M = 2^12, b = 6 and 3 (the bracket's
   far end, the strong-field regime; the pin is the light-bending note's at [1, 16384], the design note's section 6):
     b = 6: the click's pixel 0 against the control's 6 (DETECTOR; the bracket 0.5 pixel), the age at the click 99 against 89 (DETECTOR; the bracket 1 interval), 3 turn(s) of the fan on the way (GAMEBOARD)
     b = 3: the click's pixel -4 against the control's 3 (DETECTOR; the bracket 0.5 pixel), the age at the click 97 against 89 (DETECTOR; the bracket 1 interval), 3 turn(s) of the fan on the way (GAMEBOARD)
   THE SUN: k = G M / (R c^2) = 2.122e-06; the rule's bending 2 k = 0.8756 arcsec against nature's 4 k = 1.7512
   (Dyson, Eddington and Davidson 1920: 1.98 +- 0.16 and 1.61 +- 0.40; VLBI gamma = 0.99992 +- 0.00012); the rule's delay
   (G M / c^3) ln(4 r_1 r_2 / b^2), half Shapiro's (Cassini 2003: gamma - 1 = (2.1 +- 2.3) x 10^-5): PPN gamma = 0 in both.
   THE PRICE (a count per interval on series K's `mass` world; the rows from BEAM_LAW note 35 (vii): 13618 crowd rows, 447 beam rows):
     the reading of A and V at every Node with a row (5 columns, about 12 operations per row read): 168780 per interval,
     shared by every row at the Node (9192 Nodes of the box hold crowd rows);
     per row: the wall 2, the push 6, the nearest of the fan's neighbours 2 x 6, a turn 3: about 23 x 14065 = 323495;
     against today's walk, 16 x 14065 = 225040: about 3.2 times the walk's count.
